# In grammar an expletive is a word that fills a slot in a sentence
# without adding meaning. The term is unrelated to the everyday sense of
# a swear word. An expletive opener fills the subject slot with `there`
# or `it` and moves the real subject later (`There is a flag that
# disables the cache` becomes `The --no-cache flag disables the cache`,
# and `it is possible that the cache is stale` becomes `the cache might
# be stale`). Sentences built as `What X does is Y`, which grammar calls
# pseudo-clefts (`What the hook does is skip the file`), and `the reason
# is that` do the same.
#
# `there is` counts only with a relative pronoun (`that`, `which`, or
# `who` opening a description of the noun before it) one to three words
# later. Without one, `there is a lock` states that a thing exists. Each
# gap length is its own alternative, the way the not-only rule writes
# its `but` tail. `it is advisable that` stays out, because the
# obligation rule reports `it is advisable`.
#
# It hit 121 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 19 of 20 sampled hits
# rewrote with the real subject first. The miss took `that` as a
# determiner, a word that introduces a noun (`there's no value for that
# key`).
expr there [(there is), (there are), (there was), (there were), there's]
expr rel [that, which, who]
expr does [does, do, did]
expr main [(@there * @rel), (@there * * @rel), (@there * * * @rel), ([(it is), (it was), it's] <ADJ, !advisable> that), (what * @does is), (what * * @does is), (what * * * @does is), (the reason [is, was] that)]
let message "Expletive opener. Make the real subject the subject of the sentence."
let description "Flags `there is ... that`, `it is ... that`, and similar openers that delay the real subject."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The --no-cache flag disables the cache."
allows "There is a lock."
allows "The cache might be stale."
allows "It is advisable that you pin the version."
